digraph ReasoningTree {
  rankdir="TB";
  "N11" [label="(5,3,0) The inability to modify the upstream region poses a significant limitation on the editing range of existing prime editing systems"];
  "N14" [label="(8,1,0) Substituting SpyCas9 nickase (H840A) with Francisella novicida Cas9 nickase (H969A) expands the editing range of prime editing systems"];
  "N16" [label="(9,4,0) Template-jumping prime editing (TJ-PE) expands the editing range of prime editing"];
  "N18" [label="(11,6,0) Transient expression of an engineered MMR-inhibiting protein in PE4 and PE5 systems enhances the efficiency of substitution, small insertion, and small deletion prime edits by an average 7.7-fold"];
  "N20" [label="(11,3,0) Adding additional peptide or protein domains improves prime editing efficiency"];
  "N22" [label="(13,2,0) Many prime editing systems can only achieve these efficiencies within approximately a 10 bp range downstream of the pegRNA nick"];
  "N33" [label="(16,2,0) The location of nicks in two-pegRNA systems is in trans"];
  "N34" [label="(16,3,0) This configuration in two-pegRNA systems is associated with an elevated probability of inducing double-strand breaks (DSBs)"];
  "N36" [label="(17,1,0) The editable region in two-pegRNA systems is positioned in between the downstream direction of the two-pegRNA nicks"];
  "N37" [label="(18,1,0) Two-pegRNA approaches are unable to edit the upstream region of either pegRNA nick"];
  "N38" [label="(0,0,0) Abduction-reasoning: Given that two-pegRNA systems improve range but still cannot edit upstream regions, therefore upstream editing remains a fundamental unsolved problem"];
  "N4" [label="(2,4,0) The pegRNA includes a 3′ extension encoding the desired edit"];
  "N40" [label="(0,0,0) Abduction-knowledge: If upstream editing is impossible because reverse transcription only extends from nick in one direction, then enabling bidirectional editing requires modifying the nick configuration to cis arrangement"];
  "N41" [label="(0,0,0) Abduction-knowledge: If pegRNA 3′ extension determines editing range, then extending and modifying the 3′ extension could expand the effective editing range"];
  "N43" [label="(20,1,0) EXPERT uses an additional sgRNA (ups-sgRNA) located upstream of the pegRNA to generate a cis nick of the pegRNA nick"];
  "N46" [label="(22,1,0) EXPERT can efficiently perform editing on both sides of the ext-pegRNA nick"];
  "N47" [label="(22,2,0) Editing on both sides of the ext-pegRNA nick is unattainable by canonical prime editing systems"];
  "N48" [label="(22,3,0) EXPERT maintains low indel rates at the target site"];
  "N49" [label="(22,4,0) EXPERT demonstrates minimal off-target effects genome-wide"];
  "N5" [label="(3,1,0) The 3′ extension of the pegRNA contains a primer binding site (PBS) complementary to a segment of the DNA protospacer"];
  "N50" [label="(0,0,0) Induction-reasoning: Given that EXPERT demonstrates bidirectional editing capability, low indels, and minimal off-targets, therefore EXPERT successfully solves the fundamental directional limitation while maintaining safety"];
  "N8" [label="(5,1,0) Existing prime editing systems can only modify the downstream genomic region of the pegRNA nick"];
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) This paper proposes and evaluates EXPERT as a prime editing strategy that uses a cis-acting upstream sgRNA nick together with an extended pegRNA bearing an elongated 3′ extension to overcome limitations of single-pegRNA and dual-pegRNA prime editors, enabling high-purity bidirectional editing including large DNA fragments while minimizing indels and off-target effects.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) When existing single-pegRNA and dual-pegRNA prime editing approaches leave range, upstream-editing, and safety limitations, a new editor design should be developed and evaluated against those limitations."];
  "R10" [label="(0,0,0) The pegRNA 3′ extension supports prime editing by containing PBS and RTT components that help specify the desired edit."];
  "R11" [label="(0,0,0) Existing prime editing systems have an editing-range limitation because they can modify downstream genomic regions of the pegRNA nick but cannot modify upstream regions."];
  "R12" [label="(0,0,0) Even with reported range and efficiency improvements, many prime editing systems remain constrained to efficient editing within approximately 10 bp downstream of the pegRNA nick."];
  "R13" [label="(0,0,0) Remaining needs for editing beyond approximately 10 bp downstream, editing upstream of the nick, and avoiding trans-nick DSB concerns motivate a new prime editing approach."];
  "R14" [label="(0,0,0) The trans nick configuration of two-pegRNA systems is associated with an elevated probability of inducing double-strand breaks."];
  "R15" [label="(0,0,0) EXPERT implements the proposed design combination by using an upstream sgRNA to generate a cis nick and an extended pegRNA with an elongated, modified 3′ extension."];
  "R16" [label="(0,0,0) The study develops and evaluates EXPERT, a prime editing strategy that combines an upstream cis-acting sgRNA nick with an extended pegRNA to enable bidirectional editing, support large-fragment edits, maintain low indels and minimal off-target effects, and address remaining range limitations."];
  "R2" [label="(0,0,0) Several distinct prime editing strategies have been reported to expand editing range."];
  "R3" [label="(0,0,0) Multiple molecular approaches have been reported to improve prime editing efficiency."];
  "R8" [label="(0,0,0) A prime editing system that combines a cis nick configuration with an extended pegRNA could support bidirectional editing and expand the effective editing range."];
  "N36" -> "N38" [label="abduction-phenomenon"];
  "N37" -> "N38" [label="abduction-knowledge"];
  "N46" -> "N50" [label="induction-case"];
